{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqed9vz7vr9","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"SubagentStart hook does not fire for background Agent tool spawns","summary":"Empirical finding (task /29): the SubagentStart hook hard-codes agent_tool_spawn:true in its /agent/start payload, yet EVERY agent record in the live daemon (GET /agents, agents.json) shows agent_tool_spawn:null — never true, never even false. If the hook reached /agent/start for background run_in_background Agent-tool spawns, those records would carry true. They do not. Named background workers (worker-gate-tests, build2-readygate, edge-judge-subagent, etc.) all show session:null and agent_tool_spawn:null. Conclusion: the SubagentStart hook does NOT fire (or does not reach /agent/start) for background Agent-tool spawns on the desktop app. The records that DO carry a session are SessionStart-registered dispatcher records, not subagent-hook records. Consequence: the exec.js unification (honor explicit flag OR derived parent_session_id) makes the fix correct WHEN /agent/start is hit, but since the hook never fires for background spawns, a self-register-on-claim fallback (start_task registering its own agent record with agent_tool_spawn:true) is likely needed as a follow-up to actually unblock hook-less background workers. This is exactly why task /29's own start_task is refused 409.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-14T22:39:33.667Z","vec":[-0.08743541687726974,-0.009117570705711842,-0.060063332319259644,-0.0007187369628809392,0.002305596834048629,-0.04826817661523819,-0.026092393323779106,0.016825689002871513,0.0019408324733376503,0.04372187331318855,-0.008713356219232082,-0.07330100238323212,-0.011941508390009403,0.02248733676970005,0.062218423932790756,0.07022447884082794,0.1116330623626709,-0.0815853551030159,0.009248285554349422,-0.06188596040010452,-0.008595818653702736,0.008012055419385433,0.03909995034337044,-0.013498632237315178,-0.04085265472531319,-0.05220477655529976,-0.073241